cent is opposed  this increase in military spending so  Kate why are so many Germans so  reluctant to open their their pockets to  essentially spending money on their own  defense when in fact they say NATO's so  important well this is becoming a huge  bugbear and as we heard they're just in  the interview there we're here in  particularly vocal opposition and  demands from from their US and Germany  last year spends 1.2 3% of its GDP on  defense and that now the the current  budget at least plans to increase that  to 1.5 percent by 2024 but this 2  percent hurdle or target so say of GDP  spending on on defense is still a long  way away and there's actually an open  end target there in Germany but they  still as I mentioned has very much to do  with the reluctance in Germany to  participate in front-line military  operations and the SPD which was is the  minor is a junior partner in the  governing coalition right now they have  really put the brakes on defense  spending and instead they're calling for  more money to be invested into actual  military capabilities but that in itself  is a really big challenge for Germany  there's been really damning reports over  the last few months about the state of  the Bundeswehr of the German military  with lack of recruits lack of equipment  so that itself is very difficult and the  idea of even getting Germans to become  members of the military as well is a  whole different challenge as a debate  ongoing right now about whether the  Bundeswehr should be going into schools  to even promote the fact that people can  get a career there so this certainly  isn't a subject that is going to be off  the agenda anytime soon all right  the W's political correspondent Kay  Brady thank you so much to Libya now  where forces loyal to a powerful militia  leader have reached the outskirts of  Tripoli the seat of the country's  un-backed government the offensive  launched by General
 

